{"authored" [{"question" "q-277802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=36$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950745/state.json" "file_sha256" "30c5b79e9e238590162a8024f13a2b68d51e7f9716a809b4cadf712083c92a83" "basis" 590 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950745/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-277802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-4(0)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950745/state.json" "file_sha256" "30c5b79e9e238590162a8024f13a2b68d51e7f9716a809b4cadf712083c92a83" "basis" 590 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950745/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-277802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950745/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14ac6821779920b8bb5a0c599bd962881c8ed710e9269b01bc56512189f6eb3d" "basis" 590 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950745/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-277802" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" 